    "result_text": "Tests are green and the serialized event is identical in shape (`{\"seq\":1,\"ownerId\":11,\"customerCode\":\"NSW-16E6609E\",\"membershipLevel\":1,\"event\":\"OWNER_CREATED\"}`).\n\n## What I changed\n\nBoth findings were fair, and I addressed both:\n\n**1. Replaced hand-rolled JSON with Jackson.** Dropped the `toJson()` string builder and the ~30-line `quote()` char-by-char escaper. The record now serializes through a shared, thread-safe `tools.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper` (the same Jackson 3 stack the rest of the project uses), with `@JsonPropertyOrder` fixing the wire order. Jackson 3's `writeValueAsString` throws only unchecked exceptions, so no try/catch leaks in. The event class is no longer a mini JSON library.\n\n**2. Collapsed the three-way naming mismatch.** I committed to a concrete `customerCode` field, matching today's spec. The concept is now named `customerCode` in exactly one conceptual place (the component), so the factory and the serialized key both derive from it \u2014 no abstract `primaryIdentifier` field paired with a hardcoded getter and a string literal. The memberId evolution the spec hints at is documented as a single consistent rename rather than half-encoded into a field name that never reached the wire. I also folded `event` into a record component so the marker flows through the same serialization path.\n\nStructure otherwise unchanged: `OwnerCreatedEvent` remains the value type, `seq` still lives with the event it numbers as a monotonic `AtomicLong`, and `AuditOwnerCreated` still emits it after the human-readable line under the pipeline's transaction governance. Acceptance behaviour is intact \u2014 `AcceptanceTest` passes and the build is green.",
