        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe endpoint decomposition is fine \u2014 a dedicated `OwnerCreatedEvent` value type, built from the owner and emitted by the existing `AuditOwnerCreated` step, is the right place for this, and the `seq` source lives with the event it numbers. The `primaryIdentifier` abstraction is a reasonable nod to the spec's memberId evolution. But two structural issues:\n\n1. **Hand-rolled JSON serialization reinvents Jackson.** `toJson()` plus the 30-line `quote()` helper \u2014 manual string escaping, control-char `\\uXXXX` encoding, the works \u2014 is effectively a bespoke JSON string encoder living inside a domain event class. This project already serializes everything through Jackson (`ObjectResponse<T>`, the DTOs). A maintainer here would expect a structured event to be produced the same way, not via a private char-by-char escaper. This is logic in the wrong unit: the event record has quietly become a mini JSON library. Prefer a shared/static `ObjectMapper` (the record can carry `@JsonProperty` names, or build a small `Map`), which is both idiomatic and correct-by-construction, and drops `quote()` entirely.\n\n2. **The `primaryIdentifier` abstraction is only half-delivered, creating an internal naming mismatch.** The record component is `primaryIdentifier`, but `of(Owner)` hardcodes `getCustomerCode()` and `toJson()` hardcodes the literal key `\"customerCode\"`. So the same concept is named three different ways within one ~87-line class, and the abstraction that motivated the field name doesn't actually reach the serialized output. When the memberId migration lands, a maintainer still has to hand-edit both the factory and the string literal \u2014 the abstract name buys almost nothing while costing clarity. Either commit to a concrete `customerCode` field (matches today's spec, simpler) or route the JSON key through the same abstraction (e.g. a field/`@JsonProperty` name that moves with the identifier) so the name and the wire form stay in sync.\n\nNeither is a correctness issue; both are about the event class carrying more (and more inconsistent) structure than a maintainer of this codebase would find natural."
